proposal.text:
{
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "After the first requested container had been lifted, returned and released, the open hand had cleared it and needed to approach the container hiding the second requested color. The retained uncovered scene, pressed-button flag and completed-pickup count correctly identified the next target. However, the policy placed its hover about 1.1 cm from the recorded center and rotated to a square-equivalent grasp orientation substantially farther from the current hand orientation. This is an action-selection SUFFICIENCY problem, not a missing historical fact.

Strengthen the container-center reconstruction rule: distinguish the four corners of the same horizontal top face from lower side-wall corners, overhanging edges and the outer silhouette of the entire container. Intersect only those top-face corner rays with the approximately z=0.0634 plane, then average the reconstructed corners or intersect the reconstructed diagonals. Check the resulting square against all visible top edges and the available calibrated views. Do not average the whole container's image silhouette or combine corners at different heights. Use this center for the z=0.20 hover and subsequent grasp; the remembered cube location determines container identity, not its elevated grasp center.

Replace the rule choosing the square-aligned yaw closest to zero with a rule choosing the equivalent square-aligned yaw requiring the smallest wrapped rotation from the current measured TCP yaw. Preserve that alignment for descent and lift, refining it from visible world-plane edges rather than image-plane slopes. This also applies when transferring between requested containers after release clearance: do not reset the orientation preference to zero. In the failed situation, the current yaw was approximately +0.63 radians; the equivalent target alignments were approximately +0.96 and -0.61 radians, so +0.96 is the appropriate continuation. The other observed transfer likewise favors continuing toward a negative yaw rather than switching to its positive square-equivalent orientation. Keep the existing return-and-release and release-clearance priorities. No memory field or update condition changes.",
  "refused": "The report proposed specifying a particular grasp-face pair and supplied an exact target center and yaw. Do not add those coordinates as a special-case waypoint or posit a historically designated face pair: the target geometry is currently visible, and the supplied episodes do not establish such a designation. Resolve the equivalent-face choice using the current hand orientation and improve geometric reconstruction instead. The reported yaw discrepancy alone is not a failure of the stated position-and-gripper comparison, but removing the closest-to-zero rule makes the action instruction consistent with the observed transfers."
}

